<details><summary>ownHomePage trusts the raw chosen origin instead of the verified own home</summary>

📍 [`electron/main.mjs` L2558](https://github.com/kevinle128/OpenMausBot/blob/988a6db9fd34e82cff66ce0ba0bb0482eb8c0fe6/electron/main.mjs#L2558) · Security

**Problem**: `ownHomePage` used `ownHome?.origin()`. That value comes from `own-home-choice.json` whenever `home.away()` is true, and nothing checks that the saved entry is still a hand-saved server of the person's own. The gate also ignored `desktopRemoteAccess`. Nearby authority checks (`lendingTarget`, `lendableServer`, the `placePage` check in `accounts`) all use `ownHome?.home()`.

**Failing scenario**: Suppose the chosen home's saved entry is replaced by a Team Cloud entry with the same origin (`withTeam`) while `own-home-choice.json` still names it. That shared server's page then gets `window.ogb.updater` and the `update:check`/`install`/`seen` channels with no confirmation dialog. In remote-client mode, the saved home origin also keeps the bridge, although `myCloud()` returns null in that mode so the bridge stays on the local page only.

**Proposed fix**: `ownHomePage` now uses `desktopRemoteAccess ? null : ownHome?.home()?.origin`. This matches the sibling checks and keeps the existing single-line layout. The tests now give `ownHome` a `home()` method. They check that the bridge is refused when `home()` is null, meaning the entry is no longer hand-saved, even if `origin()` still returns the address, and refused under `desktopRemoteAccess`.

